    "q-130739": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130739",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Suppose $Y$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $det(Y)=2.$ What is the value of $det(-4Y)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot Y)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(Y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=-4$ and $n=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(-4Y) & =(-4)^{3}\\cdot det(Y) \\\\  & =-64\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-128.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
