    "q-42204": {
      "dom_id": "step-q42204",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=4.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}$ and ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we only have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(4) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
