    "q-72750": {
      "dom_id": "step-q72750",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "Suppose $C$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(C)=-24.$ The matrix $D$ is obtained from $C$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{5}$ and ${R}_{3}:=-\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(D)?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{5}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=-\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $-\\frac{1}{6},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $-\\frac{1}{6}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $-\\frac{1}{6}$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-det(C)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}det(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-24) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
