    "q-43047": {
      "dom_id": "step-q43047",
      "name": "Question 24",
      "problem": "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
